B.bkx=new A.iT("The Greeting Is a Qur\u2019anic Sentence","\u0627\u0644\u0633\u064e\u0644\u0627\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0643\u064f\u0645\u0652","As-salamu alaykum \u2014 peace be upon you \u2014 is a Qur\u2019anic formula: the Prophet \ufdfa is told to greet the believers who come to him with it (6:54), and the angels greet the people of Paradise with \u201cpeace be upon you for what you patiently endured\u201d (13:24). The reply, wa alaykum as-salam, mirrors it. Every time you greet a Muslim, you are speaking Qur\u2019anic Arabic.","Qur\u2019an 6:54; 13:24",null,null)
B.bkF=new A.iT("Family Words You Already Own","\u0623\u064e\u0628\u064c / \u0623\u064f\u0645\u0651\u064c / \u0627\u0628\u0652\u0646\u064c / \u0628\u0650\u0646\u0652\u062a\u064c","Ab (father), umm (mother), ibn/bint (son/daughter) \u2014 the same words that carry the Qur\u2019an\u2019s family passages: Yusuf says to his father \u201cya abati\u201d (12:4), a mother carries her child (46:15), Luqman counsels his son (31:13), and Maryam is ibnat \u2018Imran (66:12). Learn these four and the family words in many verses will already be familiar.","Qur\u2019an 12:4; 46:15; 31:13; 66:12",null,null)
B.bkA=new A.iT("The Definite Article: Al-","\u0627\u0644\u0643\u0650\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064f \u2014 \u0643\u0650\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064c",u.N,"Grammar note",null,null)
B.bkE=new A.iT("Allah Is the Name, Rabb Is the Word","\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u064f \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e","Allah is the proper Name; rabb is the noun (Lord, Nurturer). In the Qur\u2019an rabb is almost always joined to what it governs \u2014 rabbi al-\u2018alamin (Lord of the worlds), rabbuna (our Lord), rabbuka (your Lord) \u2014 rather than standing alone with al-. Watch how the Qur\u2019an uses each word.","Qur\u2019an 1:2","/quran","See it in Al-Fatiha")
B.aBB=s([B.bkx,B.bkF,B.bkA,B.bkE],t.n1)
B.bkQ=new A.y9("First Words, Real Words",B.aBB)
B.bkB=new A.iT("Al-Fatiha, Word by Word","\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0652\u062f\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650 \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e",u.gO,"Qur\u2019an 1:2","/quran","Recite it in the reader")
B.bkI=new A.iT("The Formula You Say 17 Times a Day","\u0625\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0643\u064e \u0646\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062f\u064f","Iyyaka na\u2019budu \u2014 You alone we worship. Iyyaka (You) is placed before the verb; that fronting is what gives the sentence its \u201calone\u201d. Na\u2019budu is the present-tense verb, and the na- at its start means \u201cwe\u201d. Salah is a daily Arabic lesson the body never forgets.","Qur\u2019an 1:5",null,null)
B.bkJ=new A.iT("Slow Is the Standard","\u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u062a\u0651\u0650\u0644\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u064f\u0631\u0652\u0622\u0646\u064e \u062a\u064e\u0631\u0652\u062a\u0650\u064a\u0644\u0627\u064b","And recite the Qur\u2019an with measured recitation (73:4). Slow enough to hear each letter. Arabic learned slowly stays; Arabic rushed evaporates.","Qur\u2019an 73:4",null,null)
B.aGF=s([B.bkB,B.bkI,B.bkJ],t.n1)
B.bkP=new A.y9("Reading Whole Verses",B.aGF)
B.bkz=new A.iT("The Simplest Sentences","\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u064f \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0646\u064e\u0627","Arabic needs only two words for a full sentence. Two nouns make a nominal sentence: Allahu rabbuna \u2014 Allah is our Lord (42:15), with no word for \u201cis\u201d. A verb and its doer make a verbal sentence: khalaqa Allahu \u2014 Allah created (29:44). Most sentences you will read are built from these two patterns.","Qur'an 42:15; 29:44",null,null)
B.bkK=new A.iT("The Verb: Past and Present","\u062e\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0642\u064e \u0648\u064e\u064a\u064e\u062e\u0644\u064f\u0642\u064f","Khalaqa \u2014 He created (past, 96:1). Yakhluqu \u2014 He creates (present, 28:68). The past is fixed; the present is alive. Arabic shows time in the verb's shape, not in extra words. Learn khalaqa and you have the pattern of a typical three-letter past verb.","Qur'an 96:1; 28:68",null,null)
B.bkC=new A.iT("The Feminine Marker: The Round Ta","\u0645\u064f\u0624\u0652\u0645\u0650\u0646\u064c \u0648\u064e\u0645\u064f\u0624\u0652\u0645\u0650\u0646\u064e\u0629\u064c","The round ta (\u0629) at the end of a word often marks the feminine: mu'min (a believing man) and mu'minah (a believing woman). The Qur'an uses both: \u201cit is not for a believing man or a believing woman\u201d (33:36). Note that al-Rahman and al-Rahim in the basmala are both masculine forms \u2014 the ta marker is a common sign of the feminine, not a rule without exceptions.","Qur'an 33:36",null,null)
B.bkL=new A.iT("The Broken Plural","\u0643\u064e\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064c \u2192 \u0643\u064f\u062a\u064f\u0628\u064c","Not every plural adds a sound ending. Kitab (book) becomes kutub (books) \u2014 the vowels inside change shape. This is the broken plural, and it is everywhere in the Qur'an. Learn it as a pattern to recognize, not a rule to fear.","Qur'an 2:285 (wa-kutubihi)",null,null)
B.aEO=s([B.bkz,B.bkK,B.bkC,B.bkL],t.n1)
B.bkN=new A.y9("Building Sentences",B.aEO)
B.bkv=new A.iT("I, You, He \u2014 in the Qur'an","\u0625\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0643\u064e \u0646\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062f\u064f","Ana \u2014 I. Anta \u2014 you (masculine). Anti \u2014 you (feminine). Huwa \u2014 he. Hiya \u2014 she. In Al-Fatiha you say iyyaka na'budu \u2014 You alone we worship. The -ka is \u201cyou\u201d, and the na- on the verb is \u201cwe\u201d: pronouns can ride inside and on the end of words, invisible until you learn to see them.","Qur'an 1:5",null,null)
B.bky=new A.iT("The Attached Pronoun: -ka and -ki","\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650\u064a \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0643\u064e \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0646\u064e\u0627","Rabbi (my Lord), rabbuka (your Lord, masculine), rabbuki (your Lord, feminine), rabbuna (our Lord). One word, four meanings, one small sound added. This is Arabic's economy \u2014 a single attached letter carries a whole relationship.","Qur'an 93:3 (rabbuka); 19:24 (rabbuki); 42:15 (rabbuna)",null,null)
B.bkH=new A.iT("The Dual: Two of Something","\u064a\u064e\u0648\u0652\u0645\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0646\u0650","Yawm (day) becomes yawmayni (two days) \u2014 the dual ending -ayni (or -\u0101ni when the word is the subject). The Qur'an uses it precisely: the two gardens of the man in Surah 18 (18:32), and the earth made in two days in Surah 41 (41:9). Arabic counts two as its own number, not as 'two ones.'","Qur'an 18:32 (jannatayn); 41:9 (yawmayn)",null,null)
B.aCz=s([B.bkv,B.bky,B.bkH],t.n1)
B.bkO=new A.y9("The Pronouns You Pray With",B.aCz)
B.bkD=new A.iT("The Idaafa: Possession in Two Words","\u0643\u064e\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650","Kitabu Allah \u2014 the Book of Allah. Two nouns side by side, and the relationship is possession. The first word loses its 'the' (kitabu, not al-kitabu); the second keeps it (Allah, already definite). This construction is everywhere \u2014 every 'of' in translation is often an idaafa in Arabic.","Qur'an 35:29 (kitaba Allahi)",null,null)
B.bkG=new A.iT("Names Are Already Definite","\u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064c \u0631\u064e\u0633\u064f\u0648\u0644\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650","A personal name is already definite, so it never takes al-: Muhammad, never al-Muhammad. Muhammadun rasulu Allah (48:29) is a name followed by an idaafa. A few names also drop the tanwin ending \u2014 Ahmadu in 61:6 \u2014 a pattern grammarians call a diptote. For now, just notice it.","Qur'an 48:29; 61:6",null,null)
B.bkw=new A.iT("Putting It Together: One Verse, Fully","\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0652\u062f\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650 \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e","You now know the parts of this verse: the definite article (al-hamdu, al-\u2018alamin), the prefix li- meaning \u201cbelongs to\u201d (li-llahi), the idaafa (rabbi al-\u2018alamin \u2014 Lord of the worlds), and the plural ending -in on al-\u2018alamin (a sound masculine plural). Read it again \u2014 you are not transliterating anymore. You are reading.","Qur'an 1:2","/quran","Read it in the reader")
B.aAU=s([B.bkD,B.bkG,B.bkw],t.n1)
B.bkM=new A.y9("Reading With Possession",B.aAU)
B.aLd=s([B.bkQ,B.bkP,B.bkN,B.bkO,B.bkM],A.ac("z<y9>"))
